{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789145262,"nonce":"n3reSV/VIBuNxCmHR7py76G4JPE=","title":"array comprehension with a filtering `if` pushes a default instead of skipping","message":"`[for (i in 0...10) if (i % 2 == 0) i else if (i % 3 == 0) i]` yields\n`0,0,2,3,4,0,6,0,8,9` (length 10) instead of `0,2,3,4,6,8,9` (length 7).\n\nAn `if` inside a comprehension whose branches do not cover every iteration is a\nFILTER: an iteration that matches no branch contributes NO element. Instead one\nelement is pushed per iteration, with 0 where no branch produced a value.\n\nREPRO (/tmp/probe/CMP1.hx):\n\n    class CMP1 {\n        static function main() {\n            var r = [for (i in 0...10) if (i % 2 == 0) i else if (i % 3 == 0) i];\n            trace(\"len=\" + r.length);   // 10, want 7\n            trace(r.join(\",\"));         // 0,0,2,3,4,0,6,0,8,9\n        }\n    }\n\nCompiles and exits 0 -- a silent wrong answer, not a crash.\n\nCorresponds to conformance Issue9040, though that row is currently reported as\n`uncompiled unit.issues.Issue9040.main` for an unrelated reason: it calls the\nshim's `aeq`, which is trap-stubbed, and the stub cascades to `test` and then\n`main`. Fixing this alone will not flip the row.","files":null}]}